    passage: `<b>PAIRED PASSAGES:</b> The following passages consider how ordinary objects acquire meanings beyond their practical uses.<br><br><b>Passage A</b><br><br>After the river withdrew, the community center became a temporary home for objects without owners. Volunteers carried in plastic bins filled with whatever the water had scattered: a dented saucepan, single shoes, framed photographs clouded beneath their glass. I was assigned to arrange the tables. Believing efficiency required certainty, I made signs for kitchenware, clothing, documents, and items beyond repair.<br><br>Mrs. Alvarez, who coordinated the room, moved slowly behind me. She placed a child's red mitten beside a water-stained recipe notebook rather than with the other clothes. When I pointed out the mistake, she showed me a name written inside the mitten and the same surname on the notebook. The objects, she explained, might have left one house together. Keeping them close could help someone recognize both.<br><br>My categories began to seem too confident. A brass key could be hardware, but it could also identify a house whose door was gone. A chipped blue cup belonged among dishes until a man recognized it as the pencil holder from his wife's desk. He did not take it immediately. He stood beside the table, telling us how she sharpened pencils over the sink because she disliked crumbs of graphite on paper.<br><br>By evening, the room looked less orderly but more legible. We grouped objects by uncertain connections, leaving room between clusters for later discoveries. People supplied stories that no label could have predicted. I had thought our task was to return property. Mrs. Alvarez understood that before an object could be returned, it sometimes had to return a memory.<br><br><b>Passage B</b><br><br>The paper map in my coat pocket was already obsolete when I bought it. The transit authority had released an app, and station screens announced delays more accurately than printed lines could. Still, I liked unfolding the map across my knees. Its colored routes made the city appear graspable, even when a train stopped between stations and the lights flickered.<br><br>Each crease became associated with a different trip. One corner tore during a rainstorm when I used the map as a roof for a bag of library books. A coffee ring marked the afternoon my cousin and I planned three destinations but reached only one. I wrote no notes on the paper, yet wear supplied a record more personal than any saved itinerary.<br><br>On a crowded train, a boy beside me asked whether the blue line reached the aquarium. His father was trying to enlarge a route diagram on a phone with a cracked screen. I handed them the map and traced a transfer with my finger. The boy held the sheet carefully, as if it were an official document rather than something softened by months in my pocket.<br><br>They left at the next stop, carrying the map by accident. I noticed only when the doors closed. For a moment I was irritated, then I pictured the boy opening it beneath the aquarium sign. The map had ceased recording my journeys and entered someone else's. Its usefulness, which I had treated as a private kind of memory, had become portable again.<br><br>The next morning I bought another copy from the same kiosk. It was crisp, accurate, and entirely blank in the way that mattered. I folded it along the printed lines and put it in my pocket, knowing that use would gradually replace the city's perfect geometry with a smaller history of weather, waiting, and chance exchanges.`,
