function countWords(html) {
  return html.replace(/<[^>]+>/g, " ").trim().split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;
}

const extensions = {
  "4A": "<br><br>A week later, I saw a family studying a station diagram and almost offered the new map. My hand reached toward my pocket before I remembered that generosity did not guarantee a return. I offered it anyway, but waited while they copied the route. The paper came back with one new crease, folded by a stranger along a line I would not have chosen. I left the crease in place. It was evidence that the map could gather a shared history without remaining with every person who used it.",
  "6A": "<br><br>The team also compared weekday and weekend use. Office workers favored seats with quick access to sidewalks, while weekend visitors stayed longer near shaded edges. The same bench could therefore support rapid meals on Tuesday and extended conversation on Saturday. Use reflected the interaction of design with schedules rather than a permanent identity attached to the object.<br><br>The researchers recommended testing changes incrementally. A chair moved for a week can reveal more than a costly redesign defended by assumption. Observation, adjustment, and further observation make the plaza an ongoing inquiry rather than a finished diagram.",
  "6B": "<br><br>Individual history mattered as well. Bats that had occupied one box repeatedly were somewhat more likely to return to it, even when another box offered a slightly more favorable temperature. Familiarity may reduce the time spent inspecting crevices or the risk of entering an unsafe shelter. When temperature differences became large, however, thermal conditions outweighed that tendency.<br><br>The researchers did not treat box choice as identical to natural roost choice. Rock cracks vary in depth, humidity, airflow, and access routes in ways that wooden boxes cannot reproduce. The boxes allowed controlled comparisons, but observations in natural cracks were still needed to test whether the same tradeoffs operated there.",
  "7A": "<br><br>The width of the final search also changed predictably. Ants trained on smooth, short routes searched within a smaller area than ants that crossed irregular terrain or experienced conflicting filters. Researchers could therefore use search spread as an indirect measure of uncertainty, even though the ant offered no explicit report of confidence.<br><br>Experiments under overcast skies produced another contrast. When polarized patterns weakened, ants relied more heavily on a familiar panorama near the nest. Newly displaced ants without that view searched sooner. No cue was universally dominant; reliability depended on what the environment made available and what the ant had previously learned.",
  "8A": "<br><br>Before leaving, they walked the route once more. Nia added arrows showing normal flow and photographed each valve with its label visible. Mr. Vale suggested keeping the paper map too. A phone could lose power during the next emergency, and a diagram on the wall could be read by whoever arrived first. Their two records served the same facts under different conditions.<br><br>The following morning, Nia passed Mr. Vale in the courtyard. He was tightening the hose connection he had complained about. This time his concentrated expression did not seem accusing. She asked whether the map needed a line for the outdoor tap. He handed her the blue tape.",
  "14A": "<br><br>The same principles shape composition. Writers for moving ensembles may place especially clear rhythms before difficult corners or avoid delicate entrances where the route becomes narrow. They are not merely writing a sequence of sounds; they are anticipating how information will travel among moving bodies. The route becomes one of the conditions to which the music responds.<br><br>Recordings from a single microphone can conceal this organization. A microphone near the front captures a firm lead, while one near the rear may register echoes and delayed layers. Neither position alone represents what every performer heard. Multiple recordings reveal the work of maintaining relation across distance.",
  "15A": "<br><br>Researchers improve their models by testing predictions at another bay rather than merely refining the first dataset. A relationship that appears strong at a sheltered site may weaken where storms develop faster or feeding flats vanish earlier with the tide. Successful prediction across locations suggests a general process; failure can reveal a condition the original study overlooked.<br><br>Departure is not the same as successful arrival. Some transmitters detect a bird leaving but not where it lands. Linking receiver arrays along the route helps distinguish a long migratory flight from a short relocation to another feeding area. That distinction prevents researchers from treating every nighttime movement as equivalent.",
};
extensions["6A"] += " This process remains deliberately provisional.";
